    <string name="rhythm_derive_timing">Beat grid</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_timing_tracked">Tracked</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_timing_classic">Classic</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_timing_approx">Approximate: this master stores only one grid. Regenerate the master to get the exact classic variant.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_timing_approx_short">approximate</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_timing_tracked">The tracked piecewise grid: dynamic-programming beat tracking over a 64-band novelty curve, then a locally weighted linear fit.\n\n· One anchor per tracked beat; each anchor follows the song’s real tempo changes instead of assuming a constant rate.\n· Tempo still comes from the whole-song phase-coherent refinement; the rate guard rejects a fit that strays more than 5% from it.\n\nThis is the most accurate alignment with the audio and the recommended default for new charts.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_timing_classic">The 0.4.3-pisces.1 single-point grid: beat length comes from the refined whole-song BPM, and the anchor comes from comb-folding the novelty curve plus the median residual of the strongest onsets.\n\nThe whole song rides one fixed rate that never drifts mid-song. Songs with a steady tempo land most accurately; songs that change tempo gradually drift in the later half. This variant is what reproduces old charts exactly — it is not the default for new ones.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_loudness_missing">This master was generated by an older version and stores no RMS data. Regenerate the master to use the loudness filter.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_master_line">Master: %1$d notes · BPM %2$s</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_mode">Derivation</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_mode_grid">Beat grid</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_mode_onset">Raw onsets</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_mode_classic">Classic grid</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_mode_hint">Beat grid snaps notes onto a piecewise beat grid: more musical, but timestamps move. Raw onsets keep the detected times: closer to the audio, but melodic passages can stay empty. Classic grid is the frozen 0.4.3-pisces.1-era default: one fixed-rate grid for the whole song. Select several to compare; tap the info icon after a name for the full description.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_modes_none">Pick at least one derivation mode.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_selection">Note selection</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_selection_balanced">Balanced</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_selection_melody">Melody first</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_selection_hint">Melody first reserves slots for vocals and lead instruments so drums cannot eat them. Raw-onsets derivation only.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_close">OK</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_button">More info</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_grid">Every onset in the master chart is re-placed on the beat grid, so note timestamps move.\n\n· Grid: the piecewise beat grid stored with the master chart — each segment has its own beat length and phase, following real tempo changes in the song.\n· Slots: Easy / Normal / Hard use 1/1, 1/2 and 1/4 of a beat per slot. The strongest onset in each slot wins and lands on that slot time.\n· Chords: onsets within 70 ms of the winning onset and at least 20% as strong join it as double or triple notes, up to the Notes per beat slot limit.\n· Lanes: frequency bands map onto 4 lanes, with neighbouring notes avoiding the same lane.\n\nFeel: steady, on-beat and easy to play. The cost is rewritten timestamps, so very dense percussion loses some detail.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_onset">No timestamp is moved. This mode only picks which onsets from the master chart survive, so note times match the audio exactly.\n\n· Budget: each difficulty is a density budget — Easy 2 notes/s with a 300 ms minimum gap; Normal 4 notes/s, 150 ms; Hard 7 notes/s, 75 ms.\n· Grouping: onsets within 70 ms first form one hit (chords included), then a weighted interval schedule keeps the highest-scoring set that still respects the minimum gap.\n· Trimming: a 1-second sliding window drops hits over budget; a chord counts by its number of notes.\n· Score: onset strength dominates, with a small bonus for onsets landing on a beat.\n\nFeel: closest to the ear and never out of sync with the audio; but notes are not guaranteed on a beat grid, and strong percussion can crowd out melodic phrases (see Note selection).</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_classic">Frozen replica of the 0.4.3-pisces.1 default generation logic (2026-11-01 era). It is implemented separately, so later algorithm changes cannot move it.\n\n· Grid: one fixed-rate grid for the whole song. The anchor is the selected timing’s first point and the beat is the median of that timing. Pair it with the Classic beat grid (the old single-point timing) to reproduce the old build note for note; a tracked master only gives a comparison grid.\n· Slots / chords / lanes: exactly the old quantization rules — 1/1, 1/2 or 1/4 beat per slot, the strongest onset wins a slot, onsets within 70 ms and at least 20% of its strength join as a chord, lanes follow the frequency band with same-lane avoidance.\n\nThe rate is perfectly constant and never drifts mid-song. Songs with a steady tempo land most accurately; songs that change tempo gradually drift in the later half.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_balanced">One global density budget: all onsets compete in a single pool and the strongest win. Those are usually drums and percussion, so melodic phrases get crowded out. Raw-onsets derivation only.</string>
    <string name="rhythm_derive_info_melody">The per-second budget is split into melody and percussion slots (Normal 3:1, Hard 5:2; Easy has no split), so drums cannot take the melody slots. Chords spanning both groups become two separate hits. Raw-onsets derivation only, and the default that won the listening A/B test.</string>
